Subject: [PATCH] storage: use no-op atomic writes for key files

BlueZ rewrites several storage key files even when the serialized
contents are unchanged.  On JFFS2 this creates unnecessary churn, and
the old create-then-write flow can leave an empty file behind when power
is lost between the two operations.

Add a small storage write helper that compares the existing contents
before writing.  When the data changed, write to a temporary file in the
same directory, fsync it, rename it over the target and fsync the
directory so either the old or new file survives a reboot.  Use the
helper for device storage writes, including the cache and LinkKey paths,
and avoid pre-creating empty cache/LinkKey files.
Log each storage write attempt with the target path, payload size and
whether the helper skipped it as unchanged or performed a write.

Upstream-Status: Pending

